# Skip link

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A skip link is the first link on a page. It stays out of sight until a keyboard user reaches it, then appears. Pressing it moves keyboard focus to the main content, so people do not have to tab through the header and navigation on every page.

It is a Civica pattern for standalone pages. Home Assistant has no skip link and no main landmark of its own.

This page covers:

- Skip link
  - Civica skip link pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A link with the words “Skip to main content”, placed before everything else that can take focus.
- A fragment address, such as `#main-content`, that matches an element on the same page.
- A target, normally the `<main>` element, with an `id` and `tabindex="-1"`. The `tabindex` lets the page move focus to it without adding a stop for the Tab key.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-skip-link-pattern"><code>Civica skip link pattern</code></h3>

The link is hidden until it has focus. When it has focus it shows as a block of dark text on a yellow background, with a thick yellow outline, in 16 pixel type. When pressed, a script moves focus to the target, and the target shows no outline of its own while it holds that focus. If the fragment is missing or the target is absent, the script reports an error, so check both.

Put the link before the back link and any other repeated controls. Aim it at the main content, not at the first decorative heading.

**When not to use it.** Do not add a skip link to every card, and do not point one at a fragment that does not exist. A link that only scrolls the page can leave keyboard focus at the start of the navigation.

**In Home Assistant.** A fragment link cannot reach an element inside another shadow tree, and Home Assistant draws its pages inside shadow trees. It exposes no stable main target. A Markdown card removes `id` and `tabindex`, so it cannot hold a skip link or a target. Do not claim a skip link for a dashboard. See [Markdown card](markdown-card.html).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One link removes repeated tabbing on pages with long headers.
- It works without a mouse and costs nothing for people who do not need it.
- It needs a real target on the same page, so it suits standalone pages and not dashboards.
- On a dashboard, the nearest alternatives are a short view with real headings in order, which is also a way to bypass blocks, and the navigation described in [Navigation](navigation.html).

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use one skip link, first on the page, in plain words: “Skip to main content”. It should appear when it takes focus and be clear against the page. Sticky headers must not cover the link when it appears.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks: this is the control the criterion asks for on a page with repeated blocks.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order: the next Tab after pressing the link goes to the first control in the main content.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): the revealed link must be seen and not covered.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check the revealed link in your own theme.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): “Skip to main content” says where it goes.

Short, predictable wording and one link keep the effort low.

